  "worked_solution": "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{-t}} & =L{{e}^{3t}}+L{-{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+1},\\,s>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s+1)-(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1-s+3}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$",
